d = new_doc(spacing=1.5, running="Detection of machine-written scholarly text")
TITLE(d, "Detecting machine-written scholarly text depends on the generator and on what authors know", size=16)
P(d, "[Authors, affiliations and corresponding author to be completed]", indent=False, align="center", size=11, spacing=1.15, after=10)
P(d, "**Abstract**", indent=False, after=2, spacing=1.15)
P(d, "Organizations that screen documents for machine authorship must decide how much to spend, yet detection research reports accuracy for one generator at a time, without base rates, costs or adaptive authors. We study cascaded screening: a free screen built from counts of small linguistic events decides which documents reach a costly language-model judge. Using 474 human papers and papers from GPT-4o, Claude and Llama, we find that screens detect well only within a generator, that a strong judge almost misses one generator, and that a cascade keeps most detection at lower cost when machine papers are rare. A feature-aware edit cuts the screen's detection from 53% to 8% and weakens the cascade, whereas a generic rewrite does not evade it, and neither fools the judge. A decision model explains these patterns and identifies features that are costly to edit as the basis of robust screens.", indent=False, spacing=1.3, size=11, align="justify")
P(d, "", indent=False, spacing=1.0)

P(d, "Fluent, well-organized prose has long signalled effort in scholarly publishing. Large language models make that signal cheap: a model can draft an abstract, a methods section or a plausible set of results in a minute, and the output is often good enough to survive a quick read. For publishers, preprint servers and research-integrity offices the question is therefore no longer whether machine-written papers exist but how much to spend on finding them, given that every check costs money, every flag costs an investigation and every miss carries a loss.", indent=False)
P(d, "The detection literature mostly asks a different question: how accurately a classifier separates machine from human text. Detectors based on a language model's own probabilities" + C('gehrmann','mitchell') + " and judge-style evaluation by a second model" + C('zheng') + " are usually reported for one generator at a time, without a base rate, without the cost of a false alarm or a miss, and without asking whether authors will adapt once they know how they are screened. A detector with an excellent hit rate can still be a poor investment if machine papers are rare and its false alarms swamp its detections, and a detector that works today may stop working as soon as its method is published.", indent=False)
P(d, "We study the screening decision directly, with three questions. How should an organization combine a free screen with a costly judge, as a function of the base rate, the costs and the detection rates? How well do a cheap count-based screen and language-model judges detect machine-written papers, and how well do they transfer across generators? And how much of the screen's value survives when authors adapt? We formulate the problem as a two-stage cascade, extend it to strategic authors, and calibrate both with data from three generators and two judges. The free screen is built from counts of seven small linguistic events in English scientific papers, such as parentheses, semicolons and passive clauses, which we model as a count process whose rate varies along the document. This keeps every quantity traceable to a countable feature.", indent=False)

