P(d, "We cut each document into four consecutive windows of 500 tokens and counted seven events in each (Methods). Human text is not Poisson: 49% of documents reject a Poisson null for square brackets, 42% for parentheses and 36% for semicolons, and only discourse markers behave as a Poisson process (median dispersion index 1.00, 6% rejection). A single distance from Poisson therefore has almost no discriminating power (AUC 0.54 for GPT-4o, 0.51 for Claude and 0.53 for Llama). Machine text departs from human text in both directions (Fig. 1). Nominalizations are less dispersed in all three generators (median dispersion 0.76, 1.10 and 1.27 against 1.67 in human text), and passive constructions and quotation marks are more dispersed in all three (passives 2.51, 1.79 and 2.02 against 1.20). Event rates differ even in sign across generators: Claude uses more semicolons than human authors (3.54 per 500 tokens against 1.95), whereas GPT-4o uses far fewer (0.51), so no rule based on average frequency describes machine text in general.", indent=False)
