H2(d, "A decision model explains when the screen pays")
P(d, "In the cascade model (Methods), a document with posterior probability *p* of being machine-written is worth sending to the judge if and only if *p* exceeds a threshold τ that depends on the judge's price, the cost of investigating a flag and the loss from a missed paper. Four implications follow. The depth of the cascade is nondecreasing in the base rate. A judge that cannot detect a generator lowers its value for the whole stream, because the relevant detection rate is the weighted mean across generators. The judge is never worth calling if its detection rate falls below a bound set by its price and the stakes. And the screen's main economic contribution is to raise the base rate the judge sees, which raises the precision of its flags: with a 1% base rate and a 2% false-alarm rate, only about 23% of the papers an unscreened judge flags are machine-written (77% at 10%, 93% at 30%).", indent=False)
P(d, "We calibrated the model with the measured judge price (US$0.0174 per document), false-alarm rate (2%) and detection rates, and with illustrative values for the cost of investigating a flagged paper (US$20), the loss per missed paper (US$100 or US$500) and the base rate, which our data cannot measure. Table 3 and Fig. 2 show the results. The optimal share of documents judged rises from 14% at a 1% base rate to 41% at 10% and 67% at 30%. The judge's price is a minor part of total cost, while its false alarms are not: for the equal-weight mixture at a 1% base rate, judging everything costs US$17 in judge calls but US$522 in investigations per 1,000 documents, and the cascade cuts investigations to US$102. Savings against judging everything are largest where machine papers are rare and the judge is expensive (71% at a 1% base rate with a judge 100 times pricier) and small at high base rates (1% to 5% at 30%). These values are structural illustrations and not estimates for any organization.", indent=False)
