H2(d, "A feature-aware edit defeats the screen but not the judge, and a generic rewrite does neither")
P(d, "A machine-using author in the model chooses an evasion effort *e* that lowers the probability of detection *d*(*e*) at a cost. Equilibrium effort rises with the stakes, and a screen that is very effective against non-adaptive authors can provoke enough evasion to detect less than a weaker screen (a detectability paradox). We tested this with a white-box attack on the five events that are easy to edit. For each machine paper, the attack moves a share *e* of the way from the paper's own count to the human mean count for each event, and places the edits in two of the four windows. The screen degrades quickly (Table 4): averaged over generators, the AUC falls from 0.889 to 0.664 and the share detected at a 5% false-positive rate from 53% to 8% under full attack, with half of the loss at *e* = 0.25. For Claude papers the AUC falls below 0.5, so edited Claude papers look more human than real human papers. A screen built only on the two unedited events (passives and nominalizations) is unaffected (mixture AUC 0.780 before and 0.779 after), whereas a screen built on the five edited events falls to chance (0.849 to 0.491). The robust features are weaker without an attack (0.780 against 0.889 for all seven).", indent=False)
