P(d, "A generic attack that needs no knowledge of the features did not work. Llama 3.3 70B rewrote 300 machine papers to read as if written by an experienced human researcher, at about US$0.003 per paper. Averaged over generators, the AUC rose from 0.885 to 0.914 (0.837 to 0.884 when Llama is excluded from training, because the attacker is itself a generator), and for Claude papers detection became clearly easier (AUC 0.750 to 0.905). The rewrite moved counts further from the human mean, not toward it: it removed parentheses, bracketed citations, semicolons and passives. A hybrid of rewrite followed by edit was no stronger than the edit alone (mixture AUC 0.688 against 0.675 with Llama excluded). What matters for evasion is therefore knowledge of the screen's features, not rewriting power as such.", indent=False)
P(d, "The judge was not fooled by either attack. For GPT-4o and Llama papers its AUC did not fall (paired differences from +0.004 to +0.014 for the hybrid attack and the edit alone), and for Claude papers both attacks made detection easier (+0.167 and +0.080, share detected at 5% false positives rising from 0.01 to 0.35 and 0.05). Because a rank average includes a screen the attack has made uninformative, fusion did worse under attack than the judge alone (0.937 against 0.985 for Llama papers). The cascade, which uses the screen as a gate, suffers the same way, because attacked papers fall out of the top-scoring share and never reach the judge: with 10% machine papers and 30% of documents sent to the judge, detection of Llama papers fell from 88% to 54%. Judging every document was unaffected (0.96 to 0.94). The saving of a cascade is therefore bought with a dependence on a screen that an informed author can defeat.", indent=False)

H1(d, "Discussion")
P(d, "Detection accuracy is the wrong quantity to optimize in isolation. The value of a screen depends on the base rate, on the costs of false alarms and misses, and on whether authors respond, and each of these shifts the answer in ways that headline accuracy hides. Three results stand out. First, accuracy does not transfer across generators, and a screen that needs no examples of machine text does not work in our setting, so maintenance is the dominant cost of any screen. Second, a strong judge can have an almost complete blind spot, and since an organization cannot know which generator it faces, a single blind spot drags down the relevant detection rate. Third, a screen whose features are known is fragile, and publishing the features, as research does, supplies that knowledge. This is the logic of costly signals" + C('spence','connelly') + " applied to a detector's inputs: a feature is informative only to the extent that it is costly to fake. In our data, passives and nominalizations played that role. Because we publish our method, the relevant benchmark for any deployed version is the equilibrium detection rate, not the headline accuracy reported here.", indent=False)
P(d, "The evidence has clear limits. It rests on three generators, one genre (English-language scientific papers) and two judges. Samples are modest, and the Claude sample (152) is smaller than the GPT-4o (245) and Llama (202) samples because we capped generation to limit cost; bootstrap intervals for cross-generator AUCs are about ±0.05, and the judged subset of 500 documents is small enough that the cascade and fusion results are indicative. The clustering model was designed after we had seen baseline results, and its confirmatory test uses one held-out generator. Papers were generated in four requests, which may introduce structural artifacts such as inflated passive dispersion, and human papers come from two extraction pipelines. Costs of review and of a missed paper, and the base rate, are assumed. The attackers are not the strongest conceivable: none edits passives and nominalizations or optimizes against the detector's weights, the hard-to-edit feature set was chosen because our attack left it untouched, and the generic rewrite used one attacker and one prompt. We did not compare against other detectors" + C('mitchell') + ", did not run embedding-based comparisons" + C('pillutla','zhang') + ", did not test light human editing of machine text, and did not test whether explainable evidence improves human decisions. A stronger adversary, measured cost parameters, more and newer generators, and a randomized experiment with expert reviewers would turn this pilot into a fuller test.", indent=False)

H1(d, "Methods")
H2(d, "Documents")
P(d, "Human papers were sampled at random from 2015–2019, before large language models were widely used for scientific writing: 300 from the open-access subset of PubMed Central (article XML body paragraphs) and 200 from arXiv (PDFs converted to text, reference lists removed, broken lines rejoined). We excluded documents shorter than 1,500 words and required at least four 500-token windows, leaving 474 human papers. For each sampled paper a generator wrote a paper on the same topic from the original title and abstract, with one of three prompts of increasing detail. We used GPT-4o, Claude Sonnet 5.5 and Llama 3.3 70B Instruct (open weight) through a commercial API gateway. A single request produced only about 800 words from GPT-4o, so each paper was written in four requests (introduction, methods, results, discussion with conclusion) and concatenated. GPT-4o produced 245 unique papers (median about 2,750 words), Claude 152 (4,566 words; capped to limit cost) and Llama 202 (about 2,970 words). Llama was added last, after the clustering model had been developed, and serves as the confirmatory test. Every document was truncated to the same length.", indent=False)
H2(d, "Screens")
P(d, "Each document was cut into consecutive windows of 500 whitespace-delimited tokens, and only the first four windows (2,000 tokens) were kept for every document. In each window we counted seven events: parentheses, square brackets, semicolons, paired quotation marks, fourteen discourse markers (for example *however*, *therefore*, *moreover*), passive constructions (a form of *be* plus a participle, by pattern matching) and nominalizations (words ending in *-tion*, *-ment* or *-ity*). The patterns for passives and nominalizations are approximate. For each document and event we computed the mean count per window (rate) and the dispersion index (variance over mean), and the baseline screen is a logistic regression on these fourteen quantities. For the clustering model, events are treated as a doubly stochastic Poisson process, for which the Fano factor in a window of *w* tokens is *F*(*w*) = 1 + Var(Λ_{w})/E(Λ_{w}), with Λ_{w} the rate integrated over the window. We estimated *F*(*w*) for each event at window sizes of 50, 100, 200 and 400 tokens by moment matching, clipped to [0.05, 20] and set to one when an event does not occur, and summarized each event by its rate, the mean log Fano factor across scales and the slope of log *F*(*w*) on log *w*, giving 21 features.", indent=False)
H2(d, "Judges and evaluation")
P(d, "GPT-4o and Gemini 2.5 Pro were asked, zero-shot at temperature 0 with one fixed prompt, to estimate the probability (0 to 100) that an excerpt was written by an AI language model. They saw a random sample of 500 excerpts (200 human papers and 100 from each generator), in random order with opaque identifiers, with markdown symbols and bare section titles removed. Gemini 2.5 Pro was allowed up to 8,000 output tokens; 12 of 500 answers were unreadable. A document was excluded for every method if either judge's answer was unreadable, leaving 196 human papers and 92 to 95 papers per generator. GPT-4o is also a generator, so its verdicts on GPT-4o papers involve self-recognition; Gemini is not a generator. Detection was evaluated by five-fold cross-validation in which all material from one source paper stays in one fold; we report the AUC with 95% bootstrap intervals over documents and, where noted, the share of machine papers detected when 5% of human papers are flagged. For transfer, a screen was trained on human papers and the other generators and tested on a generator it never saw. Three analyses were specified before they were run: the test of the clustering model on Llama, the comparison of screens with judges and rank-average fusion, and the cascade calibration.", indent=False)
H2(d, "Decision model")
P(d, "A share π of documents are machine-written. The screen assigns a score *s* with likelihood ratio ℓ(*s*) = *f*_{M}(*s*)/*f*_{H}(*s*), nondecreasing in *s*, so the posterior probability of machine authorship is *p* = πℓ/(πℓ + 1 − π). A judged document is flagged with probability *J* if machine-written and *a* if human. Costs are *c*_{J} per judge call, *c*_{R} per flagged paper investigated (assumed to settle its status) and *L* per unflagged machine paper. **Proposition 1:** sending a document with posterior *p* to the judge reduces expected cost by *V*(*p*) = *p*[(*L* − *c*_{R})*J* + *c*_{R}*a*] − (*c*_{J} + *c*_{R}*a*), which is linear and increasing in *p*, so the document is worth sending if and only if *p* ≥ τ = (*c*_{J} + *c*_{R}*a*)/((*L* − *c*_{R})*J* + *c*_{R}*a*); if (*L* − *c*_{R})*J* ≤ *c*_{J}, the judge is never worth calling. **Proposition 2:** the set of documents worth judging expands as the likelihood-ratio threshold falls, which it does as π rises, so the cost-minimizing depth is nondecreasing in π, in *J* and in *L*, and nonincreasing in *c*_{J} and *c*_{R}. **Proposition 3:** τ rises as *J* falls; for a stream mixing generators with weights *w*_{g}, the relevant rate is *J̄* = Σ*w*_{g}*J*_{g}. **Proposition 4:** among documents an unscreened judge flags, the machine share is π*J*/(π*J* + (1 − π)*a*). For strategic authors, the author chooses effort *e* ∈ [0, 1] at cost (κ/2)*e*² to minimize *m*·*d*(*e*) + *e*²/2, with *m* = (*B* + *P*)/κ the ratio of stakes (gain *B* if the paper passes, loss *P* if detected) to the cost of effort. **Proposition 5:** if *d* is decreasing and convex, effort is nondecreasing in *m* and equilibrium detection falls as stakes rise. **Proposition 6:** if *d*(*e*) = *d*_{0}(1 − *e*), then *e*^{*} = min(1, *m**d*_{0}) and equilibrium detection *d*_{0}(1 − *m**d*_{0}) is maximized at *d*_{0} = 1/(2*m*), so a more effective screen can detect less. **Proposition 7:** a screen built on features that cannot be edited cheaply (flat *d*_{B}) has higher equilibrium detection than a screen with *d*_{A}(0) > *d*_{B}(0) and steep *d*_{A} for every *m* above a threshold. Proofs are in the Supplementary Information.", indent=False)
H2(d, "Attacks")
P(d, "The feature-aware attack moves a share *e* of the way from a paper's own count to the human mean count for parentheses, square brackets, semicolons, quotation marks and discourse markers, placing the edits in two of the four windows, as human text clusters. It knows the features but not the detector's weights, and the detector was trained on clean text and only scores attacked texts. The generic attack asked Llama 3.3 70B Instruct to rewrite each of the 300 machine papers in the judge sample (100 per generator) so that it read as if written by an experienced human researcher, in chunks of 1,000 tokens with the target length stated. Because the attacker is itself a generator, we report both the standard protocol and one that excludes Llama from training. The hybrid attack applies the edit to the rewritten text. The Gemini judge was given the hybrid-attacked and edit-only papers (first 2,000 tokens; US$11.27 in judge calls).", indent=False)
